wrong: You need everything except the last letter: `word[:-1]` (or `word[:5]`), which is "plane". Then `+ "t"` makes "planet".

=== task: predict medium
title: Slices inside other strings
code:
```python
s = "banana"
print(s[-4:])
print(s[:3] in "bandana")
print(s[-4:] in "bandana")
print("" in s)
```
wrong[nana\nTrue\nTrue\nTrue]: "bandana" is b-a-n-d-a-n-a. "nana" would need n, a, n, a in a row, but the d is in the way, so it's False.
wrong[ana\nTrue\nFalse\nTrue]: `[-4:]` starts at the 4th character from the end and runs to the end: 4 characters, `nana`.
wrong[nana\nTrue\nFalse\nFalse]: The empty string counts as being inside every string, so `"" in s` is True.
wrong[nana\nFalse\nFalse\nTrue]: `s[:3]` is "ban", and "bandana" starts with "ban", so that line is True.
hint: Work out each slice first, then look for it inside the other string.
hint: `in` needs the characters in an unbroken row. The empty string is inside every string.
right: `s[-4:]` is the last four characters, `nana`. `s[:3]` is "ban", which starts "bandana" (True). "nana" isn't in "bandana", because the d breaks the run (False). And the empty string is inside every string (True).
wrong: Slice first, then search. `s[-4:]` → "nana". "ban" in "bandana" → True. "nana" in "bandana" → False (b-a-n-d-a-n-a has a d in the middle). `"" in s` → True.

=== task: predict hard
title: Steps and negative slices
code:
```python
